Target VM → exposes system metrics via Node Exporter

Monitoring VM → runs Prometheus + Grafana

Grafana dashboard → looks exactly like your uploaded screenshots



SECTION 1 — Create Target VM (in GCP)
gcloud compute instances create target-vm \
  --zone=us-central1-a \
  --machine-type=e2-medium \
  --tags=http-server \
  --image-family=debian-12 \
  --image-project=debian-cloud

 SECTION 2 — Install Node Exporter (on target-vm)
# SSH into target-vm
gcloud compute ssh target-vm --zone=us-central1-a

# Install Docker
sudo apt update -y
sudo apt install -y docker.io
sudo systemctl enable docker
sudo systemctl start docker

# Run Node Exporter container
sudo docker run -d \
  --name node-exporter \
  --restart always \
  -p 9100:9100 prom/node-exporter


 Verify

curl http://localhost:9100/metrics | head


You’ll see Prometheus-formatted metrics.

 SECTION 3 — Find Target VM Internal IP

From Cloud Shell:

gcloud compute instances describe target-vm \
  --zone=us-central1-a \
  --format='get(networkInterfaces[0].networkIP)'


Example → 10.128.0.16

Keep that value for the Prometheus config.

 SECTION 4 — Create Monitoring VM (Prometheus + Grafana)
gcloud compute instances create monitoring-vm \
  --zone=us-central1-a \
  --machine-type=e2-medium \
  --tags=http-server \
  --image-family=debian-12 \
  --image-project=debian-cloud


SSH into it:

gcloud compute ssh monitoring-vm --zone=us-central1-a

 SECTION 5 — Install Base Packages
sudo apt update -y
sudo apt install -y wget curl git unzip adduser libfontconfig1

 SECTION 6 — Install Prometheus
wget https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/releases/download/v2.53.1/prometheus-2.53.1.linux-amd64.tar.gz
tar xvf prometheus-2.53.1.linux-amd64.tar.gz
cd prometheus-2.53.1.linux-amd64

 SECTION 7 — Configure Prometheus
sudo nano prometheus.yml


Paste this (YAML):

global:
  scrape_interval: 15s

scrape_configs:
  - job_name: 'prometheus'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:9090']

  - job_name: 'node_exporter'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['10.128.0.16:9100']   # replace with your target-vm INTERNAL IP


Save → Ctrl + O, Enter, Ctrl + X

 SECTION 8 — Run Prometheus
nohup ./prometheus --config.file=prometheus.yml --web.listen-address=":9090" &


 Verify:

curl http://localhost:9090


Open in browser:

http://<monitoring-vm-external-ip>:9090/targets


You should see both prometheus and node_exporter as “UP”.

 SECTION 9 — Install Grafana
wget https://dl.grafana.com/enterprise/release/grafana-enterprise_11.2.0_amd64.deb
sudo apt install -y musl
sudo dpkg -i grafana-enterprise_11.2.0_amd64.deb
sudo apt --fix-broken install -y
sudo systemctl enable grafana-server
sudo systemctl start grafana-server


 Check:

sudo systemctl status grafana-server


Should show active (running).

 SECTION 10 — Open Grafana & Add Prometheus Data Source

Browser →

http://<monitoring-vm-external-ip>:3000


Default login → admin / admin → set new password.

 Add Data Source → Prometheus

URL: http://localhost:9090


Click Save & Test →  “Data source is working”.

 SECTION 11 — Import Node Exporter Dashboard

Grafana → + → Import

In “Import via grafana.com”, enter:

1860



Click Load

Select your Prometheus data source

Click Import

 You’ll get a dashboard like the screenshot you shared (CPU, Memory, Disk, Network metrics).

 SECTION 12 — Firewall Rules (GCP)

Allow Prometheus & Grafana ports from Cloud Shell:

gcloud compute firewall-rules create allow-monitoring \
  --allow=tcp:9090,tcp:9100,tcp:3000 \
  --target-tags=http-server
